  "Number": 32,
  "Question": "If \\(x_{k+1}=x_k+\\dfrac12\\) for \\(k=1,\\) \\(2,\\) \\(\\ldots,\\) \\(n-1\\) and \\(x_1=1,\\) find \\(x_1+x_2+\\cdots+x_n.\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{n+3}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{n^2-1}{2}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{n^2+n}{4}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{n^2+3n}{4}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Each step adds \\(\\frac12\\), so reaching the \\(k\\)th term takes \\(k-1\\) such steps from \\(1\\). Thus, for \\(1\\le k\\le n\\),\n\\[\nx_k=1+\\frac{k-1}{2}=\\frac{k+1}{2}.\n\\]\nLet \\(S=x_1+x_2+\\cdots+x_n\\). Add this sum to a reversed copy. In each column,\n\\[\nx_k+x_{n+1-k}\n=\\frac{k+1}{2}+\\frac{n+2-k}{2}\n=\\frac{n+3}{2}.\n\\]\nThere are \\(n\\) columns. Because we use two complete copies, this works for odd or even \\(n\\), including \\(n=1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n2S=n\\frac{n+3}{2},\n\\qquad\nS=\\frac{n(n+3)}4=\\boxed{\\frac{n^2+3n}{4}}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
